{"id":4528,"date":"2025-03-20T04:07:17","date_gmt":"2025-03-19T22:37:17","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/?p=4528"},"modified":"2025-03-20T04:07:17","modified_gmt":"2025-03-19T22:37:17","slug":"tmj-disorders-ear-pain-and-tinnitus","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/tmj-disorders-ear-pain-and-tinnitus\/","title":{"rendered":"TMJ Disorders: Understanding Ear Pain And Tinnitus"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ear pain and tinnitus are a puzzling aspect of TMJ Disorders with many issues and side effects that will push you to take a closer look and consult an expert. So let\u2019s understand these issues, their causes, symptoms, and treatment options. <\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><em>If you are looking to book a consultation with an expert, then Prof. Dr. Dhivya Dilipkumar is one of India\u2019s leading TMJ specialists with more than 15 years of field experience backing her up. Reach out to her by calling 9884 520 520 and <a href=\"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/appointment\/\">booking an appointment<\/a> for your TMJ-related issues.<\/em><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The human body is amazing and mysterious.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">With an interconnected network of nerves, muscles, and skeletons, humans have been able to achieve greatness in every aspect of life. And this interconnected network, is also the cause of yet another mystery, \u201cWhy am I feeling the pain here\u201d<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The TMJ (Temporomandibular Joint) is a joint that is located on each side of your face and connects the jawbone to the skull(temporal bone). While small in size, the TMJ plays a major role in essential activities such as chewing, speaking, and yawning.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Disorders of this joint can cause a lot of symptoms, ranging from mild discomfort to chronic pain. Among these, ear-related issues such as ear pain and tinnitus, which is a ringing or buzzing sensation in the ears, are particularly perplexing. The close anatomical relationship between the TMJ and the structures of the ear often makes it challenging to differentiate the source of discomfort.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Let\u2019s first understand TMJ, and then its connection with the ear.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Understanding TMJ and Its Function<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The TMJ is a unique and complex joint in the human body. It is classified as a synovial joint, meaning it is encapsulated in a lubricating fluid that facilitates smooth movement. What makes the TMJ unique is that it performs both hinge and sliding motions, enabling the jaw to open, close, and shift from side to side. This versatility is crucial for basic activities like eating and talking.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The TMJ is supported by several components, including:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Bones: The mandible (lower jawbone) and the temporal bone (part of the skull) come together to form the joint.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Articular Disc: A small, fibrous disc within the joint acts as a cushion, preventing bone-on-bone contact during movement.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Ligaments and Muscles: These stabilize the joint and coordinate its movement. The masseter, temporalis, and pterygoid muscles, for instance, are integral to chewing and jaw mobility.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One critical aspect of the TMJ is its proximity to the ear. The joint is located just in front of the ear canal, and its movement and function are closely tied to the surrounding auditory structures. This anatomical closeness explains why TMJ disorders often manifest symptoms in the ears, such as pain or ringing.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When functioning normally, the TMJ is almost unnoticeable. However, when the balance of its components is disrupted\u2014whether by injury, inflammation, or overuse\u2014it can lead to a cascade of problems. These include not only localized jaw pain but also systemic effects such as headaches, earaches, and even dizziness, underscoring the TMJ\u2019s broader impact on health.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>What Are TMJ Disorders?<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/tmj-symptoms-and-treatment\/\">TMJ disorders<\/a> encompass a group of conditions that affect the temporomandibular joint and the surrounding muscles and ligaments. These disorders can result in pain, dysfunction, or a combination of both, significantly impacting an individual\u2019s quality of life.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Types of TMJ Disorders<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Myofascial Pain:<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is the most common type of TMD and involves discomfort or pain in the muscles responsible for jaw movement. It often stems from muscle overuse, stress, or improper jaw alignment.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Internal Derangement:<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This occurs when the articular disc inside the TMJ becomes displaced or when the joint itself is misaligned. It can lead to restricted movement, clicking sounds, or a &#8220;locked&#8221; jaw.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Arthritis:<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Similar to arthritis in other joints, the TMJ can develop inflammation, degeneration, or erosion, particularly in older individuals or those with autoimmune conditions like rheumatoid arthritis.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Common Symptoms of TMJ Disorders<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Jaw Pain or Stiffness: Often felt in the joint itself or radiating to the face and neck.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Headaches and Migraines: Chronic TMD can contribute to tension headaches or migraines.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Clicking, Popping, or Grating Sounds: Audible noises during jaw movement are a hallmark of TMD, though they may not always indicate pain.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Difficulty in Jaw Movement: Some individuals experience limited mobility, making it hard to open or close their mouths fully.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Ear-related Symptoms: These can include ear pain, tinnitus, or a feeling of fullness in the ears, often misdiagnosed as an ear infection.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>TMJ Disorders and Ear Pain<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One of the most puzzling aspects of TMJ disorders is the ear pain they can cause. This symptom often leads individuals to seek help from ENT specialists, only to discover that the root cause lies in their jaw joint.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Why Does TMJ Disorder Cause Ear Pain?<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The TMJ is located just millimeters from the ear canal and shares several nerve pathways with the auditory system. The most significant nerve involved is the trigeminal nerve, which transmits sensory information from the face, including the jaw and the ear. When the TMJ becomes inflamed or misaligned, it can irritate the trigeminal nerve or associated structures, causing referred pain in the ear.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Differentiating TMJ-Related Ear Pain from Other Conditions<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">TMD-related ear pain can often be confused with ear infections (otitis media), sinus issues, or even neuralgia. However, there are some distinguishing features:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Lack of Fever or Infection Signs: Unlike ear infections, TMJ-related ear pain is not accompanied by fever, discharge, or hearing loss.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Jaw Involvement: Symptoms often worsen with jaw activity, such as chewing or speaking.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Associated Jaw Symptoms: Ear pain may coincide with jaw clicking, stiffness, or difficulty in movement, pointing to TMJ as the culprit.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>What is Tinnitus?<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Tinnitus, commonly described as a ringing, buzzing, or hissing sound in the ears, is a challenging condition that affects millions of people worldwide. While tinnitus is often linked to hearing loss or exposure to loud noises, it can also result from temporomandibular joint (TMJ) disorders. This surprising connection is rooted in the shared anatomy and nerve pathways between the TMJ and the auditory system.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Why Does TMD Cause Tinnitus?<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Pressure on Nerves and Blood Vessels:<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The TMJ lies near major nerves like the auriculotemporal nerve, as well as blood vessels that supply the ear. Inflammation, misalignment, or tension in the TMJ can exert pressure on these structures, leading to tinnitus.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Jaw Misalignment:<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When the TMJ is out of alignment, it can create abnormal muscle tension in the jaw and neck, which may impact auditory signals and result in tinnitus.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Neuroplasticity and Sensory Overlap:<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The brain processes sensory input from the TMJ and the ear through interconnected pathways. Disruptions in the TMJ can confuse the brain\u2019s sensory processing, leading to phantom sounds like tinnitus.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Characteristics of TMJ-Related Tinnitus<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Fluctuating Intensity: Tinnitus linked to TMJ disorders may worsen or improve with jaw movements, such as chewing, speaking, or yawning.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Associated Jaw Symptoms: Unlike tinnitus caused by hearing issues, TMJ-related tinnitus often occurs alongside jaw pain, clicking, or stiffness.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Unilateral or Bilateral: Depending on the extent of TMJ involvement, tinnitus may affect one ear or both.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Impact of Tinnitus on Quality of Life<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For individuals with TMJ-related tinnitus, the constant presence of phantom sounds can lead to frustration, difficulty concentrating, and even mental health issues like anxiety or depression. Recognizing TMJ as a possible cause of tinnitus is essential for targeted treatment and relief.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Symptoms Checklist for TMD<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Before seeking professional help, use the below points as a quick checklist:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Jaw pain, stiffness, or difficulty moving the jaw.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Clicking or popping sounds during jaw movement.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Ear pain or tinnitus.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Frequent headaches or facial pain.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li>Difficulty chewing or a misaligned bite.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While these symptoms provide clues, they are not definitive for TMD without further evaluation.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Medical Examinations<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Physical Examination:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A healthcare provider, often a <a href=\"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/tmj-specialist-in-chennai\/\">TMJ specialist<\/a> or maxillofacial specialist, will assess jaw movement, listen for joint sounds, and check for tenderness or swelling around the TMJ. This hands-on evaluation helps identify mechanical issues within the joint.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Dental Assessment:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A dentist may examine the alignment of teeth and the condition of the bite, as these factors can contribute to TMJ disorders.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Imaging Techniques:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Advanced imaging can provide detailed insights into the condition of the TMJ and surrounding structures:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>X-rays:<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Useful for detecting structural abnormalities or arthritis in the joint.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging):<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Helps visualize the soft tissues, including the articular disc, and detect disc displacement or inflammation.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>CT Scans (Computed Tomography):<\/strong> Offers a detailed view of the bone structure and can identify fractures or bone-related issues.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Lifestyle Changes for Managing TMJ Disorders<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Lifestyle modifications can play a crucial role in managing TMJ disorders and reducing associated symptoms, including ear pain and tinnitus. These changes not only help alleviate existing discomfort but also prevent future episodes by addressing everyday habits that may contribute to TMD.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Posture Improvement<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Poor posture, particularly forward head posture, places stress on the neck and jaw muscles, potentially exacerbating TMJ dysfunction. Adopting good posture can significantly reduce strain on the TMJ:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Desk Ergonomics:<\/strong> Use a supportive chair and keep your computer screen at eye level to maintain a neutral neck position.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Avoid Prolonged Slouching:<\/strong> Be mindful of your posture during activities like reading, working on a laptop, or using a smartphone.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Stretching and Strengthening Exercises:<\/strong> Engage in exercises that strengthen the neck, shoulders, and back to support a healthy posture.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Dietary Adjustments<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Certain eating habits can exacerbate TMJ pain. Modifying your diet can reduce strain on the jaw and promote healing:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Soft Foods:<\/strong> Opt for softer foods like soups, mashed potatoes, yogurt, and smoothies to minimize jaw movement.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Avoid Hard or Chewy Foods:<\/strong> Steer clear of foods like gum, nuts, and tough meats that require extensive chewing.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Smaller Bites:<\/strong> Cut food into smaller pieces to make chewing easier and less taxing on the jaw.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Stress Management Techniques<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Stress often contributes to teeth clenching or grinding (bruxism), which strains the TMJ. Incorporating relaxation techniques can help manage stress and reduce TMJ-related symptoms:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Meditation and Mindfulness:<\/strong> Practice daily meditation to calm the mind and reduce jaw tension.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Progressive Muscle Relaxation (PMR):<\/strong> Focus on relaxing each muscle group, particularly in the face and jaw.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Exercise:<\/strong> Regular physical activity can relieve overall tension and improve mental well-being.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Preventing TMJ Disorders<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Prevention is the best approach to avoiding the discomfort and challenges associated with TMJ disorders. By adopting healthy habits and being mindful of jaw health, you can significantly reduce your risk of developing TMD or experiencing a recurrence.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">1. <strong>Maintain Good Jaw Habits:<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Avoid Overuse:<\/strong> Limit habits like gum chewing, nail-biting, or pen-chewing.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Jaw Resting Position:<\/strong> Keep your jaw relaxed with lips closed and teeth apart when not speaking or chewing.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">2. <strong>Protect Against Bruxism:<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Bruxism, or teeth grinding, is a major contributor to TMJ disorders. Preventive measures include:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Use a Night Guard:<\/strong> A dentist-prescribed mouthguard can protect teeth and reduce jaw strain during sleep.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Identify Triggers: <\/strong>Stress or caffeine may exacerbate grinding. Monitor and manage these triggers to reduce occurrences.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">3. <strong>Regular Exercise and Posture Correction<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A strong and flexible musculoskeletal system supports overall joint health:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Stretching:<\/strong> Perform jaw, neck, and shoulder stretches to prevent stiffness.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Posture Awareness:<\/strong> Maintain good posture during daily activities to prevent strain on the jaw and neck.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">4. <strong>Routine Dental Checkups<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Regular dental visits to the <a href=\"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/\">best dental clinic<\/a> can help identify and address issues like misaligned teeth or bite problems early, preventing them from escalating into TMJ disorders.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">5. <strong>Stress Management<\/strong><\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Since stress is a common trigger for TMD, adopting a proactive approach to managing stress can help prevent jaw tension:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Mindfulness Practices:<\/strong> Meditation, yoga, and relaxation exercises are effective tools for stress reduction.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Healthy Lifestyle:<\/strong> A balanced diet, regular exercise, and adequate sleep contribute to lower stress levels and improved overall health.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Why choose Dr. Dhivya Dilipkumar for TMJ Disorders related to ear pain and Tinnitus?<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/dr-dhivya-dilipkumar\/\">Prof Dr. Dhivya Dilipkumar<\/a> is an orthodontist and a TMJ Pain Specialist with 15+ years of experience in the field. She is a highly skilled orthodontist with a compassionate approach and a zeal to provide her patients with the best care.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Her holistic approach is a great mix of innovative techniques and her deep understanding of the medical issues in her space. She recently completed her Advanced International Mastership in Orofacial Pain, TMJ Disorders, and Sleep Medicine, making her one of the top choices for TMD professionals in the country.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Conclusion:<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Ear Pain and Tinnitus due to TMJ disorders are some of those issues that will annoy you in almost all your day-to-day activities. They may not be particularly physically demanding or harmful, but the presence of the issue will be felt painfully every day.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is where professionals like Dr. Dhivya Dilipkumar can come in and guide you in the right direction so your everyday life is peaceful. With a career that covers more than a decade, she has seen all the different scenarios of all the various TMJ disorders, and she will be able to pinpoint the issue and give you just the right guidance that is needed in your journey to recovery.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">So take the first step today towards better ears and years!<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Take care and Godspeed!<\/p>\n\n\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>FAQs<\/strong><\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Who is the best TMJ specialist for ear pain and Tinnitus?<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dr. Dhivya Dilipkumar is one of the best TMJ Pain management specialists in the country. She ensures a customized treatment approach to her patients that is backed by years of experience and innovation in the field.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>What causes TMJ disorders?<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Teeth grinding or clenching (bruxism), jaw injuries or trauma, arthritis affecting the joint, misaligned teeth or bites, and chronic stress leading to muscle tension around the jaw are some of the common reasons that can cause TMJ disorders.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>How do TMJ disorders lead to ear pain and tinnitus?<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The temporomandibular joint is located close to the ear, and its dysfunction can cause referred pain to the ear. Additionally, TMJ disorders can affect the nerves and muscles connected to the auditory system, leading to ear pain and tinnitus (ringing or buzzing sounds).<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Can TMJ disorders heal on their own?<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Mild cases of TMJ disorders may resolve with rest and self-care, such as eating soft foods, avoiding jaw overuse, and practicing stress management. However, persistent or severe symptoms often require professional intervention, including physical therapy, dental appliances, or in some cases, surgery.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Are there specific exercises for TMJ pain relief?<\/strong><\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes, targeted exercises can help strengthen and relax the jaw muscles. Some effective ones include:<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Jaw stretches:<\/strong> Gently open your mouth as wide as comfortable, then close it slowly.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Resisted mouth opening:<\/strong> Place light pressure under your chin while opening your mouth.<\/li>\n\n\n\n<li><strong>Neck stretches: <\/strong>Stretch the neck to relieve tension in surrounding muscles.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Always consult a professional before starting a new exercise routine for TMJ disorders.<\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Ear pain and tinnitus are a puzzling aspect of TMJ Disorders with many issues and side effects that will push you to take a closer look and consult an expert. So let\u2019s understand these issues, their causes, symptoms, and treatment options. If you are looking to book a consultation with an expert, then Prof. Dr. [&hellip;]<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":2,"featured_media":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"_acf_changed":false,"footnotes":""},"categories":[48],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-4528","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","hentry","category-tmj"],"acf":[],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts\/4528","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/drdilipdentalcentre.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/post"}],"auth